# One key is now required: VITE_CARTO_API_KEY
#
# It used to be true that no API key was needed. CARTO changed that for its
# raster basemaps, and the way it failed was quiet enough to be worth recording:
# a keyless tile request still returns HTTP 200 and a valid PNG, but the image
# is a flat grey placeholder and it is byte-identical for every coordinate. A
# map built on it renders a blank viewport and nothing anywhere reports an
# error. That is how this key came to be needed -- not to remove a cosmetic
# watermark, but because the basemap was already drawing nothing.
#
# Request a key at https://carto.com/basemaps/apikey and put it in frontend/.env
# (gitignored) as:
#
#   VITE_CARTO_API_KEY=cb1_...
#
# Vite inlines VITE_* variables into the client bundle, so the value is readable
# from devtools. That is expected for a raster basemap key and is why CARTO
# publishes them rather than treating them as secrets. Restrict the key by
# domain in the CARTO dashboard; do not rely on it being confidential, and do
# not commit it.
#
# The CARTO and OpenStreetMap attribution must stay visible on the maps
# regardless of the key. See https://carto.com/attributions .
#
# Not required, and still keyless:
#   * Esri World Imagery, used for the satellite basemap.
#   * AWS Terrain Tiles elevation.
#   * EOX Sentinel-2 cloudless imagery.
#   * Overpass API, for OpenStreetMap building, road and label geometry.
#
# This file used to ask for a Google Maps JavaScript API key for the satellite
# basemap. Nothing reads VITE_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY any more: the satellite view
# uses the public Esri World Imagery tile endpoint.
#
# Backend configuration (database, Redis, signing keys) lives in the repository
# root .env.example, not here.
